[108.277262, "o", "TIMEZONE FIX SUMMARY\r\n====================\r\n\r\nPROBLEM:\r\n--------\r\nThe gen_report.py script was generating reports with UTC timestamps (e.g., 00-00-00 or 05-07-45)\r\ninstead of local JST timestamps (e.g., 09-00-00 or 14-07-44) even though:\r\n- /etc/timezone was correctly set to Asia/Tokyo\r\n- The system 'date' command showed JST time correctly\r\n\r\nROOT CAUSE:\r\n-----------\r\nThe script was setting os.environ['TZ'] = 'Asia/Tokyo' but NOT calling time.tzset() afterwards.\r\nIn Python, simply setting the TZ environment variable does NOT change the timezone used by\r\nthe datetime module. You MUST call time.tzset() to force Python to re-read and apply the\r\ntimezone configuration.\r\n\r\nWhen cron runs scripts, it often starts with TZ=UTC or no TZ set. Without tzset(), Python\r\ncontinues using whatever timezone it initialized with, ignoring the os.environ['TZ'] change.\r\n\r\nTHE FIX:\r\n--------\r\nAdded two lines to gen_report.py:\r\n1. import time  (at the top)\r\n2. time.tzset()  (immediately after os.environ['TZ'] = tz_name)\r\n\r\nTEST RES"]
[108.277318, "o", "ULTS:\r\n-------------\r\nOLD behavior (without tzset):\r\n  TZ=UTC python3 gen_report.py.backup\r\n  → Generated: report_2026-05-05_05-07-45.txt (UTC time)\r\n\r\nNEW behavior (with tzset):\r\n  TZ=UTC python3 gen_report.py\r\n  → Generated: report_2026-05-05_14-07-44.txt (JST time - correct!)\r\n\r\nThe fix ensures that regardless of the initial TZ environment variable,\r\nthe script will correctly use Asia/Tokyo time as configured in /etc/timezone.\r\n\r\nREADY FOR PRODUCTION:\r\n--------------------\r\nThe script is now fixed and will work correctly in tomorrow's 6am cron job.\r\n"]
